> Why the shortest residual path grows after a blocking phase

Let old BFS levels be ell. Every residual arc already present before the
phase satisfies ell(v)<=ell(u)+1. Any newly positive cancellation arc
reverses a used level arc, so its old level change is minus one. Thus a
new residual path cannot increase the old level by more than one per
arc. A new s-to-t path no longer than the old sink level would have to
advance exactly one old level at every step. The blocking flow has removed
all such paths, a contradiction. Hence the next reachable sink distance
strictly increases. A simple residual path has at most V−1 arcs, so there
are at most V−1 successful phases in exact arithmetic, followed by one
failed BFS. In the executed cancellation example those distances are
three, five, then unreachable.

The implementation uses an explicit path stack instead of recursive DFS.
Each vertex’s current-arc pointer advances permanently through its adjacency
within a phase. Successful path prefixes may be tested again, while an
exhausted arc or dead end is not retried in that phase. If J counts current
pointer advances and L counts successful path-arc updates, the recorded
DFS inspections satisfy inspections=J+L. At most 2E pointer advances occur
per phase. Every positive path push saturates a level arc, and cancellation
arcs go backwards in the fixed levels, so at most E pushes are needed in
one phase. With each path length at most V−1, a phase costs O(VE), and
all phases cost O(V^2 E), after O(V+E) initialization. Ordinary working
storage is O(V+E), including the explicit stack.

[Dinic’s original paper](https://www.cs.bgu.ac.il/~dinitz/D70.pdf)
gives the polynomial operation bound. The report’s current-arc counters
measure this concrete iterative variant; they are not elapsed-time or
arbitrary-precision bit-complexity measurements.

> Recover the classic value 23 and check the cut independently

The ten-arc six-vertex network used by the existing tests has maximum
flow 23. We now run it in the report itself. The final residual source
side is S={0,1,2,4}; crossing original arcs have capacities 12,7,4,
whose sum is 23. The table retains every original arc’s individual flow,
including the antiparallel pair between vertices 1 and 2.

For any feasible flow and any source/sink-separating cut S, summing
conservation over S cancels internal arcs and gives

\[
|f|=\sum_{u\in S,v\notin S}f_{uv}-\sum_{u\notin S,v\in S}f_{uv}
\le\sum_{u\in S,v\notin S}c_{uv}=c(S,\bar S).
\]

At exact residual termination, every outgoing crossing arc is saturated
and every incoming crossing flow is zero, since either would otherwise
create a residual arc leaving S. Equality therefore proves optimality.
This is the [flow/cut bound of Ford and Fulkerson](https://doi.org/10.4153/CJM-1956-045-5).
The separate core check computes edge-bound errors, intermediate-vertex
conservation, terminal/value agreement and the signed cut-minus-value gap.
It does not call Dinic, search for a replacement cut or repair the result.
Default absolute tolerance is zero; positive tolerances are numerical
agreements and keep the actual error and gap visible.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dinic_classic_cut.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<dm::algorithms::CapacityEdge> edges{{0,1,16},{0,2,13},{1,2,10},{2,1,4},{1,3,12},{3,2,9},{2,4,14},{4,3,7},{3,5,20},{4,5,4}};
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::dinic_max_flow_trace(6,edges,0,5);const auto c=dm::algorithms::max_flow_certificate(6,edges,0,5,t.result);
  if(t.result.max_flow!=23 || !c.optimal_within_tolerance)return 1;
  std::print("Classic flow {:.0f}; cut side {}; crossing original arcs {}; cut capacity {:.0f}.\n\n",t.result.max_flow,t.result.min_cut_source_side,c.crossing_edges,c.cut_capacity);
  std::print("| edge | from | to | capacity | flow |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<edges.size();++i){const auto [u,v,capacity]=edges[i];std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} |\n",i,u,v,capacity,t.result.edge_flow[i]);}
  std::print("\nCapacity violation {:.0f}; conservation error {:.0f}; value balance error {:.0f}; signed duality gap {:.0f}; feasible {}; optimal {}.\n",c.max_capacity_violation,c.max_conservation_error,c.value_balance_error,c.duality_gap,c.feasible,c.optimal_within_tolerance);
  std::print("BFS sink levels: ");for(const auto& phase:t.phases)std::print("{} ",phase.sink_level);std::print("(6 means unreachable).\n");
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(60,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle side;side.width=3.5;side.height=2.1;side.font_size=23;
  d.node("s",{0,0},std::format("Source side S\n{}",t.result.min_cut_source_side),side).node("t",{12,0},"Complement of S\nvertices 3 and 5",side);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle gate;gate.width=3;gate.height=1.5;gate.font_size=22;
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<c.crossing_edges.size();++i){const auto e=c.crossing_edges[i];const auto [u,v,capacity]=edges[e];const auto id="edge"+std::to_string(e);
    d.node(id,{6,3-3.0*i},std::format("{} → {}\nflow {:.0f} / capacity {:.0f}",u,v,t.result.edge_flow[e],capacity),gate).edge("s",id).edge(id,"t");}
  d.text({6,5.2},"Computed cut capacity 12 + 7 + 4 = 23 equals the feasible flow",24)
   .text({6,-5.1},"Each gate is one original crossing arc; the full ten-arc network is in the table",20)
   .save_svg("build/dinic_classic_cut.svg");
}
```
