| supplied record | tolerance | flow | cut | conservation error | value error | signed gap | feasible | optimal within tolerance |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- |
| single edge | 0.0 | 7.0 | 10.0 | 0.0 | 0.0 | 3.0 | true | false |
| single edge | 0.0 | 11.0 | 10.0 | 0.0 | 0.0 | -1.0 | false | false |
| single edge | 0.5 | 9.5 | 10.0 | 0.0 | 0.0 | 0.5 | true | true |
| broken conservation | 0.0 | 3.0 | 3.0 | 1.0 | 1.0 | 0.0 | false | false |

Expected rejections: history cap 1; total overflow 1; lost residual progress 1; unreachable nonfinite edge 1.
Iterative chain: 20000 edges, flow 1, longest pushed path 20000, BFS arc visits 40001, DFS arc tests 40000.
\image(figures/dinic_checks.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A deliberately supplied invalid result has a zero scalar cut gap but fails conservation and terminal balance. Executed controls also check tolerance semantics, trace caps, arithmetic failures and a 20,000-edge iterative path.)
\alttext(Source, middle and sink boxes show three units sent into the middle but only two leaving, exposing the one-unit conservation error despite a zero reported cut gap.)

> Verification and limits

Native tests enumerate all 729 three-vertex networks with each directed
non-loop capacity in {0,1,2}, from all six ordered terminal pairs, and all
4096 four-vertex unit/zero-capacity networks from all twelve terminal
pairs. These 53,526 source/sink computations match independently enumerated
cut minima. Plain and traced per-edge flows and cut sides agree. A separate
replay checks every BFS level, path bottleneck, residual operand/update,
blocked level graph and inspection identity against the owned original input.

The original single edge and classic value 23, the cancellation example,
parallel/antiparallel arcs, self-loops, scale changes, exact/one-short
history caps, malformed supplied certificates, complete input guards and
arithmetic failures receive explicit controls. Generated path counts and
phase counts match their closed forms, and a 30,000-edge native test
uses the explicit stack without vertex-depth C++ recursion. The report
itself runs the 20,000-edge version. All inputs are small educational
networks or canonical generated fixtures; no external dataset is required.

The mathematical flow/cut proof and phase bound hold in exact arithmetic.
The implementation uses checked doubles and numerical diagnostics; a wide
capacity range may require rescaling or a different arithmetic model.
Within the exhibited representable cases, the trace exposes how blocking
and cancellation achieve the result, and the independent flow/cut check
establishes the reported numerical agreement.

> References

- E. A. Dinic (1970), “Algorithm for solution of a problem of maximum flow
  in a network with power estimation”, Soviet Mathematics Doklady 11,
  1277–1280. [Original English translation](https://www.cs.bgu.ac.il/~dinitz/D70.pdf),
  [author’s publication list](https://www.cs.bgu.ac.il/~dinitz/pub.html).
- L. R. Ford Jr. and D. R. Fulkerson (1956), “Maximal flow through a network”,
  Canadian Journal of Mathematics 8, 399–404.
  [Original paper](https://doi.org/10.4153/CJM-1956-045-5).
